# Backend Development Rules & Standards for Gyaani App

## 🎯 Universal Architecture Principles (MANDATORY)

### Core Fundamentals (Framework Independent)
These principles apply regardless of the framework or technology stack:

#### 1. Separation of Concerns
```
┌─────────────────┐    Request Flow    ┌─────────────────┐
│   Presentation  │ ──────────────────→ │   Business      │
│   Layer         │                     │   Logic Layer   │
└─────────────────┘                     └─────────────────┘
                                                │
                                                ▼
┌─────────────────┐    Data Flow       ┌─────────────────┐
│   Data Access   │ ←────────────────── │   Data Storage  │
│   Layer         │                     │   Layer         │
└─────────────────┘                     └─────────────────┘
```

#### 2. Dependency Inversion
- High-level modules don't depend on low-level modules
- Both depend on abstractions
- Abstractions don't depend on details

#### 3. Single Responsibility
- Each module has one reason to change
- Each class has one responsibility
- Each function does one thing

#### 4. Open/Closed Principle
- Open for extension
- Closed for modification
- Use interfaces and abstractions

## 🏗️ Layered Architecture Implementation (MANDATORY)

### Universal Layer Responsibilities

#### 1. Presentation Layer
**Purpose**: Handle external communication
**Responsibilities**:
- HTTP request/response handling
- Input validation
- Authentication/authorization
- Response serialization

#### 2. Application Layer
**Purpose**: Orchestrate use cases
**Responsibilities**:
- Business workflow coordination
- Transaction management
- Event publishing
- Use case execution

#### 3. Domain Layer
**Purpose**: Core business logic
**Responsibilities**:
- Business rules
- Domain models
- Domain services
- Business validation

#### 4. Infrastructure Layer
**Purpose**: External system integration
**Responsibilities**:
- Database operations
- External API calls
- File system operations
- Message queuing

### Layer Communication Rules
```
┌─────────────────┐
│  Presentation   │
└─────────────────┘
         │
         ▼
┌─────────────────┐
│  Application    │
└─────────────────┘
         │
         ▼
┌─────────────────┐
│    Domain       │
└─────────────────┘
         │
         ▼
┌─────────────────┐
│ Infrastructure  │
└─────────────────┘
```

**Rules**:
- Layers can only depend on layers below them
- No circular dependencies
- Use interfaces for layer boundaries
- Dependency injection for loose coupling

## 📁 Module Organization Standards (MANDATORY)

### Universal Module Structure
```
src/
├── modules/
│   ├── shared/                 # Shared utilities
│   │   ├── contracts/         # Common interfaces
│   │   ├── utils/             # Utility functions
│   │   ├── constants/         # Application constants
│   │   └── types/             # Common types
│   ├── feature-module/
│   │   ├── contracts/         # Feature interfaces
│   │   │   ├── entities.ts    # Domain entities
│   │   │   ├── repositories.ts # Repository interfaces
│   │   │   └── services.ts    # Service interfaces
│   │   ├── domain/            # Business logic
│   │   │   ├── entities/      # Domain models
│   │   │   ├── services/      # Business services
│   │   │   └── validators/    # Business rules
│   │   ├── infrastructure/    # External concerns
│   │   │   ├── repositories/  # Data access
│   │   │   ├── external/      # External APIs
│   │   │   └── messaging/     # Event handling
│   │   ├── application/       # Use cases
│   │   │   ├── commands/      # Write operations
│   │   │   ├── queries/       # Read operations
│   │   │   └── handlers/      # Use case handlers
│   │   └── presentation/      # API layer
│   │       ├── controllers/   # Request handlers
│   │       ├── routes/        # Route definitions
│   │       ├── middleware/    # Request processing
│   │       └── serializers/   # Response formatting
│   └── core/                  # Core application
│       ├── config/           # Configuration
│       ├── database/         # Database setup
│       ├── logging/          # Logging setup
│       ├── security/         # Security setup
│       └── startup/          # Application startup
```

## 🏗️ Core Design Patterns (MANDATORY)

### 1. Repository Pattern
**Purpose**: Abstract data access logic
**Benefits**: Testable, maintainable, database-agnostic

```typescript
// Universal Interface (Framework Independent)
interface Repository<T, ID> {
  create(entity: T): Promise<T>;
  findById(id: ID): Promise<T | null>;
  findAll(filters?: Partial<T>): Promise<T[]>;
  update(id: ID, updates: Partial<T>): Promise<T | null>;
  delete(id: ID): Promise<boolean>;
}
```

### 2. Service Pattern
**Purpose**: Encapsulate business logic
**Benefits**: Reusable, testable, single responsibility

```typescript
// Universal Service Interface
interface Service<T, CreateDto, UpdateDto> {
  create(data: CreateDto): Promise<T>;
  findById(id: string): Promise<T | null>;
  findAll(filters?: any): Promise<T[]>;
  update(id: string, data: UpdateDto): Promise<T | null>;
  delete(id: string): Promise<boolean>;
}
```

### 3. Factory Pattern
**Purpose**: Create objects without specifying exact classes
**Benefits**: Flexible object creation, easy testing

```typescript
// Universal Factory Interface
interface Factory<T> {
  create(config: any): T;
  createFromType(type: string, config: any): T;
}
```

## 🗄️ Data Management Patterns (MANDATORY)

### Universal Data Access Pattern

#### 1. Entity Design
```typescript
// Universal Entity Interface
interface Entity {
  id: string;
  createdAt: Date;
  updatedAt: Date;
  isActive: boolean; // For soft delete
}

// Domain Entity
interface User extends Entity {
  email: string;
  firstName: string;
  lastName: string;
  role: UserRole;
}
```

#### 2. Repository Implementation
```typescript
// Universal Repository Interface
interface Repository<T extends Entity> {
  create(entity: Omit<T, 'id' | 'createdAt' | 'updatedAt'>): Promise<T>;
  findById(id: string): Promise<T | null>;
  findAll(filters?: Partial<T>): Promise<T[]>;
  update(id: string, updates: Partial<T>): Promise<T | null>;
  delete(id: string): Promise<boolean>;
  softDelete(id: string): Promise<boolean>;
}
```

#### 3. Data Validation
```typescript
// Universal Validation Interface
interface Validator<T> {
  validate(data: unknown): ValidationResult<T>;
}

interface ValidationResult<T> {
  isValid: boolean;
  data?: T;
  errors?: ValidationError[];
}

interface ValidationError {
  field: string;
  message: string;
  code: string;
}
```

### Database Design Principles
- Use consistent naming conventions
- Implement proper indexing
- Use soft delete pattern
- Include audit fields (createdAt, updatedAt)
- Normalize data appropriately

## 🔒 Security & Authentication Standards (MANDATORY)

### Universal Security Principles

#### 1. Authentication Strategy
```typescript
// Universal Authentication Interface
interface AuthenticationService {
  login(credentials: LoginCredentials): Promise<AuthResult>;
  logout(token: string): Promise<void>;
  validateToken(token: string): Promise<AuthContext | null>;
  refreshToken(refreshToken: string): Promise<AuthResult>;
}

interface AuthContext {
  userId: string;
  email: string;
  role: string;
  permissions: string[];
  sessionId: string;
}

interface AuthResult {
  accessToken: string;
  refreshToken: string;
  expiresAt: Date;
  user: User;
}
```

#### 2. Authorization Patterns
```typescript
// Universal Authorization Interface
interface AuthorizationService {
  hasPermission(userId: string, permission: string): Promise<boolean>;
  hasRole(userId: string, role: string): Promise<boolean>;
  canAccessResource(userId: string, resource: string, action: string): Promise<boolean>;
}

// Role-Based Access Control
interface Role {
  name: string;
  permissions: Permission[];
}

interface Permission {
  resource: string;
  actions: string[];
}
```

#### 3. Input Validation
```typescript
// Universal Validation Rules
interface ValidationRules {
  required: boolean;
  type: 'string' | 'number' | 'boolean' | 'email' | 'uuid';
  minLength?: number;
  maxLength?: number;
  pattern?: RegExp;
  custom?: (value: any) => boolean;
}

// Security Headers
interface SecurityHeaders {
  'Content-Security-Policy': string;
  'X-Frame-Options': string;
  'X-Content-Type-Options': string;
  'Strict-Transport-Security': string;
}
```

### Password Security
- Use strong hashing algorithms (bcrypt, Argon2)
- Implement salt rounds (minimum 12)
- Enforce password complexity rules
- Implement password reset functionality

### Rate Limiting
- Implement per-endpoint limits
- Use sliding window algorithm
- Store limits in cache/database
- Different limits for authenticated vs anonymous users

## 🌐 API Design Principles (MANDATORY)

### RESTful API Standards

#### 1. Resource Naming
```
✅ GOOD:
GET    /api/users
POST   /api/users
GET    /api/users/123
PUT    /api/users/123
DELETE /api/users/123

❌ BAD:
GET    /api/getUsers
POST   /api/createUser
GET    /api/user/123
POST   /api/updateUser/123
POST   /api/deleteUser/123
```

#### 2. HTTP Status Codes
```typescript
// Universal Status Code Usage
const HTTP_STATUS = {
  // Success
  OK: 200,                    // GET, PUT success
  CREATED: 201,              // POST success
  NO_CONTENT: 204,           // DELETE success
  
  // Client Error
  BAD_REQUEST: 400,          // Invalid input
  UNAUTHORIZED: 401,         // Authentication required
  FORBIDDEN: 403,            // Insufficient permissions
  NOT_FOUND: 404,            // Resource not found
  CONFLICT: 409,             // Resource conflict
  UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY: 422, // Validation error
  
  // Server Error
  INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR: 500, // Server error
  SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE: 503,   // Service down
} as const;
```

#### 3. Response Format
```typescript
// Universal Response Structure
interface ApiResponse<T> {
  success: boolean;
  data?: T;
  error?: {
    code: string;
    message: string;
    details?: any;
  };
  pagination?: {
    page: number;
    limit: number;
    total: number;
    totalPages: number;
  };
  meta?: {
    timestamp: string;
    requestId: string;
    version: string;
  };
}

// Success Response
const successResponse: ApiResponse<User> = {
  success: true,
  data: user,
  meta: {
    timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
    requestId: 'req-123',
    version: '1.0.0'
  }
};

// Error Response
const errorResponse: ApiResponse<null> = {
  success: false,
  error: {
    code: 'USER_NOT_FOUND',
    message: 'User with specified ID not found',
    details: { userId: '123' }
  },
  meta: {
    timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
    requestId: 'req-123',
    version: '1.0.0'
  }
};
```

### API Versioning
```typescript
// URL Versioning (Recommended)
/api/v1/users
/api/v2/users

// Header Versioning
Accept: application/vnd.api+json;version=1

// Query Parameter Versioning
/api/users?version=1
```

## 🚨 Error Handling & Logging (MANDATORY)

### Universal Error Handling

#### 1. Error Hierarchy
```typescript
// Base Error Class
abstract class ApplicationError extends Error {
  abstract code: string;
  abstract httpStatusCode: number;
  public timestamp: Date;
  public requestId?: string;

  constructor(message: string, requestId?: string) {
    super(message);
    this.name = this.constructor.name;
    this.timestamp = new Date();
    this.requestId = requestId;
    Error.captureStackTrace(this, this.constructor);
  }

  abstract toJson(): ErrorResponse;
}

// Specific Error Types
class ValidationError extends ApplicationError {
  code = 'VALIDATION_ERROR';
  httpStatusCode = 400;
  
  constructor(message: string, field: string, requestId?: string) {
    super(message, requestId);
    this.field = field;
  }
  
  toJson(): ErrorResponse {
    return {
      code: this.code,
      message: this.message,
      field: this.field,
      timestamp: this.timestamp.toISOString(),
      requestId: this.requestId
    };
  }
}

class NotFoundError extends ApplicationError {
  code = 'NOT_FOUND';
  httpStatusCode = 404;
  
  toJson(): ErrorResponse {
    return {
      code: this.code,
      message: this.message,
      timestamp: this.timestamp.toISOString(),
      requestId: this.requestId
    };
  }
}
```

#### 2. Centralized Error Handling
```typescript
// Universal Error Handler Interface
interface ErrorHandler {
  handle(error: Error, context: ErrorContext): Promise<void>;
}

interface ErrorContext {
  requestId: string;
  userId?: string;
  endpoint: string;
  method: string;
  timestamp: Date;
}

// Error Handler Implementation
class GlobalErrorHandler implements ErrorHandler {
  async handle(error: Error, context: ErrorContext): Promise<void> {
    // Log error
    await this.logError(error, context);
    
    // Send to monitoring service
    await this.sendToMonitoring(error, context);
    
    // Send alerts if critical
    if (this.isCritical(error)) {
      await this.sendAlert(error, context);
    }
  }
}
```

### Logging Standards

#### 1. Log Levels
```typescript
enum LogLevel {
  ERROR = 'error',     // System errors, exceptions
  WARN = 'warn',       // Warning conditions
  INFO = 'info',       // General information
  DEBUG = 'debug',     // Debug information
  TRACE = 'trace'      // Detailed trace information
}

// Universal Logger Interface
interface Logger {
  error(message: string, context?: LogContext): void;
  warn(message: string, context?: LogContext): void;
  info(message: string, context?: LogContext): void;
  debug(message: string, context?: LogContext): void;
  trace(message: string, context?: LogContext): void;
}

interface LogContext {
  requestId?: string;
  userId?: string;
  endpoint?: string;
  method?: string;
  duration?: number;
  metadata?: Record<string, any>;
}
```

#### 2. Structured Logging
```typescript
// Universal Log Entry Format
interface LogEntry {
  timestamp: string;
  level: LogLevel;
  message: string;
  context: LogContext;
  metadata?: Record<string, any>;
}

// Example Log Entry
const logEntry: LogEntry = {
  timestamp: '2024-09-05T17:30:00.000Z',
  level: LogLevel.INFO,
  message: 'User created successfully',
  context: {
    requestId: 'req-123',
    userId: 'user-456',
    endpoint: '/api/users',
    method: 'POST',
    duration: 150
  },
  metadata: {
    userRole: 'admin',
    ipAddress: '192.168.1.1'
  }
};
```

## 📊 Database Standards

### Schema Design
- Use Mongoose for MongoDB operations
- Define interfaces extending `Document`
- Include `timestamps: true` for createdAt/updatedAt
- Use proper indexing for performance
- Implement soft delete with `active: boolean` field

### Repository Pattern
- Create repository classes for all database operations
- Use static methods for repository operations
- Implement data conversion between DB and application formats
- Handle database errors gracefully

### Connection Management
- Use connection pooling and caching
- Implement proper error handling for connection failures
- Use environment-based configuration for database URLs

## ⚡ Performance & Scalability (MANDATORY)

### Universal Performance Principles

#### 1. Caching Strategy
```typescript
// Universal Cache Interface
interface CacheService {
  get<T>(key: string): Promise<T | null>;
  set<T>(key: string, value: T, ttl?: number): Promise<void>;
  delete(key: string): Promise<void>;
  clear(): Promise<void>;
  exists(key: string): Promise<boolean>;
}

// Cache Key Patterns
const CacheKeys = {
  USER: (id: string) => `user:${id}`,
  USER_TASKS: (userId: string) => `user:${userId}:tasks`,
  API_RATE_LIMIT: (ip: string) => `rate_limit:${ip}`,
  SESSION: (sessionId: string) => `session:${sessionId}`
} as const;
```

#### 2. Database Optimization
```typescript
// Universal Query Optimization
interface QueryOptimization {
  // Indexing Strategy
  indexes: {
    single: string[];
    compound: string[][];
    text: string[];
    unique: string[];
  };
  
  // Query Patterns
  patterns: {
    findById: string;
    findByUser: string;
    pagination: string;
    search: string;
  };
  
  // Performance Monitoring
  monitoring: {
    slowQueryThreshold: number;
    maxQueryTime: number;
    connectionPoolSize: number;
  };
}
```

#### 3. Async Processing
```typescript
// Universal Async Processing Interface
interface AsyncProcessor<T> {
  process(data: T): Promise<void>;
  retry(data: T, maxRetries: number): Promise<void>;
  schedule(data: T, delay: number): Promise<void>;
}

// Background Job Interface
interface BackgroundJob {
  id: string;
  type: string;
  payload: any;
  status: 'pending' | 'processing' | 'completed' | 'failed';
  attempts: number;
  maxAttempts: number;
  createdAt: Date;
  updatedAt: Date;
}
```

### Scalability Patterns

#### 1. Horizontal Scaling
- Stateless application design
- Load balancer configuration
- Database sharding strategies
- Microservice architecture

#### 2. Vertical Scaling
- Memory optimization
- CPU optimization
- Database connection pooling
- Resource monitoring

### Performance Standards
- Always use async/await for database operations
- Implement proper error handling with try-catch
- Use Promise.all() for parallel operations
- Avoid blocking the event loop
- Implement connection caching for databases
